:0.8638,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2020,"team":"JAX","distance":0.887,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2019,"team":"CIN","distance":0.91,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Lou Anarumo"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Browns went 3-14 in 2024 and were outscored 435 to 258. The defense did most of the work, finishing 18th in EPA allowed while the offense ranked 32nd in EPA per play. Their most distinctive traits were the highest 10 personnel usage and the highest eight man box rate in the league.","offense":"The Browns offense struggled, ranking 25th or worse in all five main efficiency measures. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Schematically, they stood out for the highest 10 personnel usage and the fewest backs in the backfield in the league. Jameis Winston ran the passing game, with Jerry Jeudy leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Browns defense was about average by EPA allowed per play. It was much better against the run than the pass, ranking 14th and 24th. Myles Garrett was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Za'Darius Smith, who had 5 sacks despite playing only 58% of the snaps. In terms of scheme, they had the highest eight man box rate in the league.","special":"The Browns were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 67% of field goals, which was the worst in the league. Their salary cap was spread across positions in a fairly typical way. Their running backs were among the oldest in the league.","players":"The quarterback job was split, with Jameis Winston and Deshaun Watson both getting meaningful snaps. Jerry Jeudy was the clear top target with 23% of the looks and 1,229 receiving yards. The backfield was a committee led by Jerome Ford and Nick Chubb. On defense, Myles Garrett led the team with 14 sacks and Denzel Ward picked off 2 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2024 Cowboys under Mike McCarthy and the 2024 Giants under Brian Daboll. Their defense looked most like the 2021 Panthers and the 2023 Lions under Aaron Glenn.","trends":"The offense went from 28th in EPA per play in 2023 to 32nd in 2024, a modest decline. The defense moved from 1st in EPA allowed per play in 2023 to 18th in 2024, a big step back. The biggest change in approach was pass rate, which went from 56% to 65%.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2024. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}